.TH XYMONAPI 8 "November 13, 2026" "xymon-plugins"
.SH NAME
xymonapi \- read-only HTTP and JSON interface to the Xymon server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B "$XYMONHOME/ext/xymonapi/xymonapi.sh"

.SH DESCRIPTION
xymonapi answers HTTP requests with what the Xymon server knows: the status
board, the alarms on it, its hosts, the values in its RRD files and the
graphs drawn from them, and the history of colour changes with the status
text saved at each. Answers are JSON, except for graphs, which are PNG.
It changes nothing in Xymon: it only reads.

It is optional, and shipped disabled. It is started by
.I xymonlaunch(8)
from the task file \fBext/xymonapi/tasks.cfg\fR, once
\fB./install.sh enable-server xymonapi\fR has linked it from
\fBtasks.d/xymonapi\fR beside tasks.cfg; its installation makes no link.
xymonlaunch starts it once xymond is running, and restarts it if it stops.

It needs Xymon 4.3.31 or later: its settings go in xymonserver.d/, which
Xymon reads from 4.3.31. On 4.3.30 a file there is ignored, password
included, and xymonapi then answers anyone; the installation refuses an
older Xymon unless given --force.

Unlike the rest of Xymon, xymonapi needs Python 3.9 or later, with the
modules fastapi, uvicorn and yaml (requirements.txt beside it lists them),
importable by the user Xymon runs as. When one is missing it logs which, and
exits with status 77.

The OpenAPI description of every operation is served at /openapi.json, and
is the file openapi.yaml beside the program.

It has two doors. The port, 127.0.0.1:1986 by default, is for other
machines, through the web server or an SSH tunnel: every request there
needs a user's password or a client token (TOKENS). The Unix socket,
$XYMONVAR/xymonapi/api.sock by default, is for the programs of the server
itself: none is needed there, because only the Xymon user and its group
may open it (XYMONAPI_SOCKET under ENVIRONMENT).

.SH OPERATIONS
Every path is under /xymon/api/v1. Only GET is accepted, but for POST on
/ask and POST and DELETE on /tokens: another method answers 405, and any
other path 404. A failure is answered with JSON, {"error": E, "detail": D},
where E is one of bad_request (400), unauthorized (401), not_found (404),
method_not_allowed (405), too_many (429) and upstream (502), and D says why. Those, and
each path's answers, are openapi.yaml's, installed beside the API.
.IP "\fB/health\fR"
Whether xymond answers a ping: {"alive":true,"server_version":"xymond ..."}
when it does, and {"alive":false} when the ping fails -- xymond is down, or
XYMSRV or XYMON is wrong. Like every path but /plugins, it answers 401 on
the port without a user, or a client token, when XYMONAPI_PASSWD is set.
Its "version" field is the version of the code this process runs, read
when it started -- an install it was not restarted for does not change it:
the version it was installed from, its
release tag, xymonapi/vX.Y.Z, when the install ran on one, else the X.Y.Z
of its PLUGIN file; it is absent when the API was not installed.
xymonapi-connect(1) compares it with the client's own.
Its "search" field describes the index /search reads -- how many passages
of manual pages and of history, when it was built -- and is null when there
is none.
.IP "\fB/states\fR [\fBentity\fR=HOST] [\fBtest\fR=TEST] [\fBverdict\fR=VERDICT]"
The status board, one state per host and test. The colour is given as a
verdict: green is ok, yellow warning, red critical, blue disabled, clear
nodata and purple unknown.
.IP "\fB/alarms\fR"
Every state whose verdict is not ok.
.IP "\fB/entities\fR"
Each host on the board, once.
.IP "\fB/pages\fR"
The pages of
.I hosts.cfg(5),
which xymond does not keep: the top page with its subpages inside it, each
with its name, its title, its path from the top, and its groups; a group
with its title, its hosts in the file's order, and the columns of a
group-only or a group-except; a title line as the pretitle of the page,
group or host after it. The hosts a page lists before any group are a group
with no title. Beside them, by host name, what the file says of a host: the
NAME, COMMENT and DESCR tags, and the pretitle. A host with nodisp is in no
group. As
.I xymongen(1)
does, a subpage before any page and a subparent whose parent page has not
come yet are left out, and the hosts after them stay where they were. The file
is asked of xymond with its \fBconfig\fR command, which gives
$XYMONHOME/etc/hosts.cfg with the \fBinclude\fR and \fBdirectory\fR lines
followed, so it is the one xymond runs with. A \fBdispinclude\fR line, which
only xymongen follows, is not: the pages of such a file are not in the
answer. xymond answers \fBconfig\fR to the addresses it takes status
messages from, so an xymond started with \fB\-\-status\-senders\fR must allow
this machine's.
.IP "\fB/status\fR \fBentity\fR=HOST \fBtest\fR=TEST"
The current status of HOST's TEST, from xymondboard: its verdict, when it
last changed (time) and was last reported (updated), and its text -- what
the web page shows, written by the monitored host or the test. When it is
acknowledged -- until the acknowledgement's end time -- or the test disabled
-- by hand, or blue from a DOWNTIME tag in hosts.cfg -- that is said too,
with the words given and until when. The command line that set the status
is left out of the text, as the web page leaves it out. HOST and TEST are
taken as given, not as patterns: 404 when the board has no such status. At
most 1 MiB of text.
.IP "\fB/search\fR \fBq\fR=QUESTION [\fBkind\fR=docs|history] [\fBk\fR=N]"
The N passages (5 by default, 20 at most) of Xymon's manual pages and of
its history nearest QUESTION by meaning, best first, from the index that
.I xymonapi-rag(1)
builds: the task tasks.d/xymonapi-rag, once a day. Each passage says where
it comes from -- page, section and item, or host, test and time -- and a
history passage carries the status text the monitored host sent, marked as
data, not instructions. The index is read again when the file changes, so
the task needs no restart of the API. 404, saying so, when there is no index
or the modules xymonapi-rag needs are missing: search is optional, and the
other operations need neither.
.IP "\fB/plugins\fR"
The code of the plugins installed on this server, for other machines to
install from at this server's versions: a gzipped tar rooted at
xymon-plugins/, laid out as the repository is -- plugins/NAME for each
plugin installed here, without its tests, with install.sh and LICENSE.
\fB./install.sh install-tools NAME\fR there installs that plugin.
Each plugin's \fB./install.sh install-server\fR stages its directory in
$XYMONHOME/ext/xymon-plugins-dist/, which is what /plugins packs, and its
\fB./install.sh uninstall-server\fR takes it out; this API names no plugin. It is the one
path open without a user when XYMONAPI_PASSWD is set: it holds the
plugins' code and nothing else -- no user, password, token or setting --
though a stranger learns from it which plugins, at which versions, run
here. It answers not_found when nothing is staged. Whoever controls this
server, or the certificate a machine trusts for it, controls the code that
machine installs from here.
.IP "\fB/series\fR \fBentity\fR=HOST \fBtest\fR=TEST [\fBmetric\fR=DATASET] [\fBfrom\fR=TIME] [\fBto\fR=TIME]"
The values of one dataset of the RRD file TEST.rrd of HOST, under
$XYMONRRDS, read with rrdtool. TEST is the file's name, not the column on
the status board: xymond_rrd names the files after the data it keeps, so
the conn test is in tcp.conn.rrd and the ssh test in tcp.ssh.rrd, cpu load
is in la.rrd and memory in memory.rrd, and a disk is in disk,<mount with
commas for slashes>.rrd. DATASET is a column in that file: la is the
load average in la.rrd, sec the response time in a tcp.*.rrd, lambda the
value in the files xymond_rrd makes from a status text's NAME : value
lines. Left out, it is the file's only dataset -- as most files hold one
-- or lambda among several; with several and no lambda, the answer is a
400 that names them. A missing file or dataset is answered 404; from after
to is a 400. xymond_rrd keeps updates for up to 30 minutes before
writing them, unless run with --no-cache; when the range reaches into that
time, /series first asks it to write HOST's, as a graph shown on the web
pages does, through its control sockets, rrdctl.* in XYMONTMP (or
XYMONRUNDIR). The step in progress may still be null, which is not a test
that stopped; without those sockets the file is read as it is. To see
what a host has, list $XYMONRRDS/HOST/ there, or run rrdtool info on a file.
.IP "\fB/graphs\fR \fBentity\fR=HOST \fBtest\fR=TEST [\fBfrom\fR=TIME] [\fBto\fR=TIME]"
The graph
.I showgraph.cgi(1)
draws for HOST and TEST, fetched from showgraph.sh, its wrapper, at
$XYMONSERVERCGIURL -- prefixed with $XYMONWEBHOST when that is a path, as
xymonserver.cfg ships it.
.IP "\fB/events\fR [\fBentity\fR=HOST] [\fBtest\fR=TEST] [\fBsince\fR=TIME] [\fBlimit\fR=N] [\fBbefore\fR=SECONDS]"
The colour changes xymond_history recorded in $XYMONHISTDIR/allevents, the
newest first: when, the verdict before and after, and how long the one
before had lasted. N is 1 to 1000, 100 by default. With before, only the
changes older than that time: the epoch of an answer's last item asks for
the page after it. The file is read from its end, so the newest changes
cost the same however long the history. A server where nothing has changed
colour yet has none.
.IP "\fB/histlog\fR \fBentity\fR=HOST \fBtest\fR=TEST \fBtime\fR=SECONDS"
The status text xymond_history saved under $XYMONHISTLOGS when HOST's TEST
changed colour at that time, the epoch of an /events item; 404 when none was
saved. At most 1 MiB of it.
.LP
A TIME is \fBnow\fR, seconds since the epoch, or a time relative to now such
as \fB-30m\fR, \fB-24h\fR, \fB-7d\fR or \fB-2w\fR. \fBfrom\fR defaults to
-24h and \fBto\fR to now. A host or test name that is not one Xymon uses,
or a time it cannot read, is answered with 400. A test name may hold a
colon, as an HTTP test's RRD file does (tcp.http.www.example.com:8080,);
a host name may not.

.IP "\fBPOST /ask\fR {"question": Q, "session": NAME, "agent": NAME}"
A question in words, answered in words by
.I xymonapi-agent(1)
installed beside the API: it runs an agent CLI the admin installed as the
Xymon user -- Claude Code, Codex or opencode -- with the Xymon MCP server,
.I xymonapi-mcp(1),
as its only tool, reading this API through its own socket. The answer is
{"answer": A, "agent": NAME, "backend": NAME}, with "session" as sent when
one was. Not there -- 404 as any unknown path -- unless XYMONAPI_ASK is set;
set with no xymonapi-agent beside the API, 404 saying so. Nothing in Xymon
changes: the agent is read-only, as every path here.

"session", letters, digits, dots, underscores or hyphens, continues a
conversation: it is the caller's, prefixed with USER@CLIENT -- the token's
user and machine, the user and "password", or "local@local" through the
socket -- before the agent sees it, so no other caller continues it.
"agent", xymon by default, names one of the server's agents, as the Xymon
user's profiles for xymonapi-agent(1) define them.

A question costs time and money where a GET does not, so a caller asks one
at a time, and at most XYMONAPI_ASK_PER_HOUR in an hour; another answers
too_many (429) with a Retry-After header in seconds. A question longer than
XYMONAPI_ASK_MAX characters is bad_request. The agent failing, or giving
no answer in XYMONAPI_ASK_TIMEOUT seconds, is upstream (502), the detail
its last line. Each question is logged with the caller, agent, backend and
how long it took; the question itself is not.

.SH TOKENS
With XYMONAPI_PASSWD set, a user may get a token for one machine and send it
instead of the password: Authorization: Bearer TOKEN.
xymonapi-connect(1) gets one, naming the machine by its short hostname, so
the password stays off the laptop. A token opens every path but /tokens, which
takes the password: a stolen token cannot make others, or renew itself.
.IP "\fBPOST /tokens\fR {"client": NAME}"
A new token for the caller on NAME -- letters, digits, . _ or -, 64 at most
-- replacing any the caller had there, which is refused from then on. It is
shown this once: only its SHA-256 is kept, in $XYMONAPI_TOKENS.
.IP "\fBGET /tokens\fR"
The caller's tokens: each machine's name and when its token was made.
.IP "\fBDELETE /tokens/\fRNAME"
Revokes the caller's token on NAME.
.LP
Through the socket they are refused, as to a token: they say who the
caller is, which the socket does not.
A user sees and revokes only their own tokens. An admin revokes anyone's by
removing that line from $XYMONAPI_TOKENS: one JSON object per token, with
its user, machine and SHA-256. Removing the user from XYMONAPI_PASSWD
revokes all of theirs: the first of their tokens used after that is refused
and every line of that user deleted, so a user put back, or a new one of the
same name, gets tokens anew by running xymonapi-connect(1). A token is refused,
and nothing deleted, while the file cannot be read.
The machine's name is the laptop's word, not
proof: it names the token, and two machines of one name share one.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
The task file loads xymonserver.cfg, and xymonapi uses these settings from it:
.IP "\fBXYMSRV\fR"
The Xymon server to ask. It is required; xymonapi does not fall back to
127.0.0.1.
.IP "\fBXYMON\fR"
The xymon client program.
.IP "\fBXYMONRRDS\fR"
Where the RRD files are, for /series.
.IP "\fBXYMONSERVERCGIURL\fR, \fBXYMONWEBHOST\fR"
Where showgraph.cgi is, for /graphs. The first is usually a path, /xymon-cgi;
the second, http://$XYMONSERVERWWWNAME, then gives the host, and must carry
the port when the web server is not on 80.
.IP "\fBXYMONHISTDIR\fR, \fBXYMONHISTLOGS\fR"
Where xymond_history keeps the events and the saved status texts, for
/events and /histlog.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_RAG_INDEX\fR, \fBXYMONAPI_RAG_MODEL\fR, \fBXYMONAPI_RAG_OFFLINE\fR"
The index /search reads, and the embedding model, as for xymonapi-rag(1),
which writes the index with the same settings from the same file.
.LP
Twelve settings are its own. Put them in a file under
\fBxymonserver.d/\fR beside xymonserver.cfg, which reads its files, for
instance xymonapi.cfg:
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_LISTEN\fR"
The address and port to listen on, as HOST:PORT or [ADDRESS]:PORT for IPv6.
The default is 127.0.0.1:1986, which only this machine reaches.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_SOCKET\fR"
The Unix socket for the programs of this machine: $XYMONVAR/xymonapi/api.sock
by default, none when set to the empty string. A request through it needs
no password and no token. The API makes it mode 660 in a directory of mode
750, so that only the Xymon user and the members of its group can open it,
and that is the whole check: the kernel's, which no header or address
could stand in for. A person at a shell is one of that group, or runs the
client as that user. The port is as it was: a request there, through the
web server or not, needs the password or a token. The socket speaks plain
HTTP whether or not the port speaks TLS, since nothing of it leaves the
machine. The clients take it as \fBXYMONAPI_URL=unix:\fRPATH, and by
default when XYMONVAR from xymonserver.cfg leads them to it, as the tasks
have it: xymonapi-mcp(1), xymonapi-rag(1).
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_PASSWD\fR"
An htpasswd file: every request on the port but /plugins needs a user and
password from it, or a client token, and is answered 401 otherwise. The default is Xymon's own,
$XYMONHOME/etc/xymonpasswd -- the file the web pages use, whose users
useradm.cgi and chpasswd.cgi manage -- so the API asks for what the pages
ask for with nothing set. When that file is missing, xymonapi does not
start, and says so: set XYMONAPI_PASSWD to another file, or to the empty
string for an API open to anyone who can reach it. The password is
checked by Apache's htpasswd program, which must be on PATH; xymonapi does
not start if it is not, or if it cannot read the file.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_TOKENS\fR"
The file of client tokens (TOKENS above), $XYMONVAR/xymonapi/tokens by
default. The API creates it, mode 600, in a directory of mode 750: the
socket's, whose group reads neither the file nor the lock.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_TLS_CERT\fR, \fBXYMONAPI_TLS_KEY\fR"
A certificate and its key, PEM: with them the API speaks HTTPS on its port,
and plain HTTP gets no answer. The defaults are xymond's own,
$XYMONHOME/etc/tls/xymond.pem and xymond.key as
.I xymon-tls(7)
sets them up, so a server with TLS for the Xymon protocol has it for the
API too, one certificate for both. Without the files the API speaks plain
HTTP. A certificate without its key, or a key that others can read, stops
it from starting. Clients that do not trust the certificate must be given
it, or the CA that signed it.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_HIDE\fR"
A file of regular expressions, one per line, # for a comment. Whatever
they match is replaced by [hidden] in every text the API returns: a
status, an acknowledgement or disabling, a saved status -- and so in the
search index built from them. A process list can show a password typed on
a command line; a line such as \fBpassword=\\S+\fR keeps it off the API,
whatever the web page shows. Read at start: an expression that is not one,
or a file that cannot be read, stops the API from starting, since running
without the hiding asked for would be worse than not running.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_ASK\fR"
Set to 1, /ask is there; unset, empty, 0, no, off or false, it is not.
It needs xymonapi-agent installed beside the API (\fB./install.sh install-server xymonapi-agent\fR),
an agent CLI installed and logged in as the Xymon user, and the socket
(XYMONAPI_SOCKET), through which the agent reads this API. Which CLI,
model and agent answer are xymonapi-agent(1)'s own settings,
XYMONAPI_AGENT_BACKEND and the rest, which go in the same file and reach
it through the API's environment; its profiles are the Xymon user's. Check
it as that user: \fBxymonapi-agent doctor\fR.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_ASK_PER_HOUR\fR, \fBXYMONAPI_ASK_TIMEOUT\fR, \fBXYMONAPI_ASK_MAX\fR"
For /ask: questions a caller may ask in an hour, 60, 0 for no limit; seconds
to wait for the agent, 300; the longest question, 2000 characters.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_PYTHON\fR"
The Python to run the API with. Unset, the launcher takes the python3 of
the virtualenv beside the plugins, $XYMONHOME/ext/venv, which \fB./install.sh
venv\fR makes with the modules the installed plugins need; without one, the
first of python3, python3.14 down to python3.9, found on PATH. When that
one lacks a module, the API says which on
stderr, the task's log, names \fB./install.sh venv-server\fR, and exits with 77.

.SH FILES
.IP "\fB$XYMONHOME/ext/xymonapi/\fR"
The program: xymonapi.sh, the launcher the task runs; app.py, xymon.py and
tokens.py, the server; xymonapi_client.py, the client the other plugins
read it with; openapi.yaml, the description served at /openapi.json;
requirements.txt, the Python modules it needs; and VERSION, what /health
gives.
.IP "\fB$XYMONHOME/ext/xymonapi/tasks.cfg\fR"
The xymonlaunch task, on when \fB$XYMONHOME/etc/tasks.d/xymonapi\fR links to
it. A later installation leaves it as it is, and whether it is on.
.IP "\fB$XYMONHOME/etc/xymonapi-apache.conf\fR, \fBxymonapi-nginx.conf\fR, \fBxymonapi-lighttpd.conf\fR"
Snippets that serve the API through the web server, for other machines:
/xymon/api/ passed to 127.0.0.1:1986, in the HTTPS part where the server
has one. Include the one for your web server where its Xymon configuration
is. Installed once; a later installation leaves them as they are.
.IP "\fB$XYMONHOME/etc/xymonserver.d/\fR"
A file of yours here holds the settings of its own listed under
ENVIRONMENT. Nothing is installed there.
.IP "\fB$XYMONVAR/xymonapi/tokens\fR"
The client tokens' SHA-256s, one JSON line each (TOKENS above).
.IP "\fB$XYMONVAR/xymonapi/api.sock\fR"
The Unix socket (XYMONAPI_SOCKET above), while the API runs.
.IP "\fB$XYMONSERVERLOGS/xymonapi.log\fR"
The log, written through the task's LOGFILE. A missing Python or module is
reported here, and so is each token made, revoked, or used: user@machine.
Every answer carries an X-Request-Id header -- the client's own, when it
sends a valid one, or one the API makes -- and every line about a request
shows it in brackets; each failure is logged once, with its status. A
client's error that names its request id is found here by it.
.LP
$XYMONHOME/etc and $XYMONHOME/ext are links to wherever an installation keeps
those directories, /etc/xymon for instance.

.SH NOTES
A failure of the server's own -- xymond not answering, rrdtool missing --
is a 502. Its answer names the reason when the text is xymonapi's own, as
"XYMONRRDS is not set"; a program's error text, which can hold paths and
settings, goes to the log instead, and the answer says so.

xymonapi needs nothing central -- no directory, no certificate authority
it must reach, no name service -- so that it answers when the rest of the
network is in trouble: Xymon is what one looks at then. Its password file
and its certificate are Xymon's own, files on the server.

From other machines, use HTTPS: with xymond's certificate the API speaks it
itself (XYMONAPI_TLS_CERT), and where the web server has a certificate the
snippets under FILES pass /xymon/api/ to the API over it. An SSH tunnel to
127.0.0.1:1986 is encrypted too, with nothing to set up. A client is given
the URL that reaches the API, scheme, host and port, without /xymon/api/v1,
which it adds: https://HOST:1986 for the API's own HTTPS; the web server's,
https://HOST, or https://HOST:PORT when its HTTPS port is not 443, for the
snippets; http://127.0.0.1:1986 through the tunnel. Plain HTTP across
a network carries the password and the status texts readable, as port 1984
does; keep it to a network where the Xymon pages themselves are plain and
trusted. On the server itself nothing need cross a network: the socket is
there. Either way, what must not leak are secrets: the password file,
which only the Xymon user reads, the key, and what XYMONAPI_HIDE blanks
out of the texts.

Requests are served from a thread pool: a slow one holds up itself alone.

.SH "SEE ALSO"
xymonlaunch(8), tasks.cfg(5), xymonserver.cfg(5), xymon(1), showgraph.cgi(1),
xymon(7), xymon-tls(7), xymonapi-connect(1), xymonapi-mcp(1), xymonapi-rag(1)
